<article class="env-proposition" id="3C2E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/3C2E">Proposition <span data-tag="3C2E">6.1.5.3</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-category. Suppose that $\calX $ satisfies the $\infty $-categorical Giraud axioms: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
The $\infty $-category $\calX $ is presentable. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(ii)$"><p>
Colimits in $\calX $ are universal. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iii)$"><p>
Coproducts in $\calX $ are disjoint. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iv)$"><p>
Every groupoid object of $\calX $ is effective. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
  <p> Then there exists a small $\infty $-category $\calC $ which admits finite limits, and an accessible left exact localization functor $\calP (\calC ) \rightarrow \calX $. In particular, $\calX $ is an $\infty $-topos. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-topos. According to Proposition <a href="/tag/AFC3" data-tag="AFC3">5.4.7.4</a>, there exists a regular cardinal $\tau $ such that $\calX $ is $\tau $-accessible, and the full subcategory $\calX ^{\tau }$ spanned by the $\tau $-compact objects of $\calX $ is stable under finite limits. Let $\calC $ be a minimal model for $\calX ^{\tau }$, so that there is an equivalence $\Ind _{\tau }(\calC ) \rightarrow \calX $. The proof of Theorem <a href="/tag/4097" data-tag="4097">5.5.1.1</a> shows that the inclusion $\Ind _{\tau }(\calC ) \subseteq \calP (\calC )$ has a left adjoint $L$. The composition of $L$ with the Yoneda embedding $\calC \rightarrow \calP (\calC )$ can be identified with the Yoneda embedding $\calC \rightarrow \Ind _{\tau }(\calC )$, therefore preserves all limits which exist in $\calC $ (Proposition <a href="/tag/DA1B" data-tag="DA1B">5.1.3.2</a>). Applying Proposition <a href="/tag/F966" data-tag="F966">6.1.5.2</a>, we deduce that $L$ is left exact, so that $\Ind _{\tau }(\calC )$ is a left exact localization (automatically accessible) of $\calP (\calC )$. Since $\calX $ is equivalent to $\Ind _{\tau }(\calC )$, we conclude that $\calX $ is also an accessible left exact localization of $\calP (\calC )$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>